    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Bump to a pinned upstream master snapshot: 0.3.3+git20250825.b1f568d
(commit b1f568d1b57cc973a1d2ee49be68d0d748d164e4, 2025-08-25).

Supersedes sr#1374753, which carried only the Requires: soapy-sdr change
on top of the 0.3.3 tag. That change is preserved here unchanged.

Why a snapshot. 0.3.3 is still upstream's newest tag and there are no
GitHub releases at all; master is exactly 12 commits ahead. The reason to
take them is commit 148c1eec, &quot;Add enhanced tuning range for RTL-SDR Blog
V4&quot;: on a Blog V4 (tuner R828D, USB strings &quot;RTLSDRBlog&quot; / &quot;Blog V4&quot;) the
module now reports a 0 Hz - 1764 MHz range instead of the generic 24 MHz
lower bound, so the dongle's built-in upconverter is reachable through
SoapySDR. The V4 is a very common stick and Factory users have no support
for this today. The other commits: a time/tick accounting fix, an
uninitialised tunerGain fix, a missing &lt;cstring&gt; include, testmode listed
in ArgInfoList, optional PLL dithering control, and CI/CMake housekeeping.
The CMake housekeeping raises cmake_minimum_required to 2.8.12...3.10 -
still under 3.5, so the -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 workaround has
to stay. Note this puts openSUSE ahead of the pack: Fedora and Void both
still ship the 0.3.3 tag. The V4 support is the reason to be there.

Testability, plainly. I have no V4 dongle, so the tuning-range behaviour
is NOT exercised - I am relying on what upstream's commit does and says.
What is verified: it builds clean natively on aarch64 for both
openSUSE_Tumbleweed and 16.0 with rpmlint 0 errors / 0 warnings / 0
badness, and the code path is present in the shipped module (the strings
&quot;RTLSDRBlog&quot; and &quot;Blog V4&quot; appear in librtlsdrSupport.so). The PLL
dithering control is compiled out here: CMake's check_function_exists
gates it on rtlsdr_set_dithering, which Factory's librtlsdr 2.0.3 does
not have (&quot;Looking for rtlsdr_set_dithering - not found&quot; in the log).

Versioning. '+git', not the '~git' that a snapshot scaffold defaults to:
'~' sorts BELOW the plain tag and the bump would be a downgrade.
rpm.labelCompare: 0.3.3+git20250825.b1f568d &gt; 0.3.3 &gt;
0.3.3~git20250825.b1f568d, and a future 0.3.4 still sorts above the
snapshot. This also matches Factory's own soapy-sdr, which has shipped
0.8.1+git20250223.6e99da1 for months. Note that a version string of the
form 0.3.3.0.20250825 seen in trackers is another distribution's
snapshot-date packaging, not an upstream release.

Carried over from sr#1374753: Requires: soapy-sdr on the module
subpackage. libSoapySDR is what dlopens the module - SoapySDR_loadModules
is a GLOBAL FUNC in libSoapySDR.so.0.8.1 - and that library always arrived
via the automatic soname dep (libSoapySDR.so.0.8-3). What the module never
pulled in is the soapy-sdr package itself, whose only content is
/usr/bin/SoapySDRUtil. So this is a usability dependency, not a functional
one: a module nothing on the system can enumerate or probe is dead weight.
Prompted by the review comment on sr#1374694.

Also in this request: pkgconfig(SoapySDR) &gt;= 0.4.0, the floor CMakeLists
actually checks for; Source switched to a pinned obs_scm service; URL
points at the repo rather than the wiki; copyright header.</comment>
